      "dom_id": "question-154330",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $8>-3y-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the addition principle, adding $1$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & >-3y-1 \\\\ 8+1 & >-3y-1+1 \\\\ 9 & >-3y+0 \\\\ 9 & >-3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $-3.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-3y \\\\ \\frac{9}{-3}\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{-3y}{-3} \\\\ -3\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{\\cancel{-3}y}{\\cancel{-3}} \\\\ -3\\, & <\\,\\,y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\ny>-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-3.$",
